\f0\fs24 \cf0 Storytelling with Tableau \
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Today we\'92ll be exploring customer distribution, value and claims through tableau visualisations \
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We have been provided a dataset which includes many forms of information. Most notably, regional demographics, customer vehicle information and various claim details. This has been explored to extrapolate some insights and further findings. \
\pard\tx566\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
Choropleth Map \
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Firstly we was asked to explore and display the distribution of our customers across the states we work in. \
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We created a choropleth map that shows where are customers are situated and then, by the intensity of the the colour, how much. While we have chosen to leave the actual numbers of the map, these are available by hovering over each state. That said, we can clearly see that California \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 represents the largest share of the customer base. \
\pard\tx566\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
Regression Plot\
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Secondly, we were asked to examine the relationship between Customer Value and Income, to see if any further insights could be drawn.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}For this, I created a regression plot. As you can see from the visualisations the trend line runs flat, showing that there is no correlation between a customers income and their value. Another point, worth mentioning is that I did remove an outlier that completely manipulated the visualisation of the graph\
\pard\tx566\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
Boxplot \
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Finally, we explored the claims for customers depending on size of vehicle. The data source provided the vehicles in three different categories, so we used the Vehicle Size category and worked from there. \
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}We found that - using the median - the most common vehicle size for a claim was smaller automobiles. Followed by Large and then midsize. \
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}However, each category had many, many outliers and the largest total claim amount actually came from a midsize vehicle. Which indicates that while there are some general averages we could follow, it would be best advised to work on each case individually. \
}